A57 — nice -20 when RLIMIT_NICE allows it, without CAP_SYS_NICE

gamescope only lowers its nice value when it holds CAP_SYS_NICE. An Android
app never does, so every gamescope thread ran at nice 0 ("No CAP_SYS_NICE,
falling back to regular-priority compute and threads") while the game's own
threads ran at nice -5 and -2 and Wine's audio threads at -19. On a Retroid
Pocket 6 (Android 13, Dave the Diver at a 60 cap, real-vsync daemon) about 1 %
of frames missed their vblank: 23 of 25 late posts over 44 s were gamescope
draw spikes, gamescope's own commit coming 3.9 to 10.8 ms late because
gamescope-xwm was runnable but not scheduled for 1 to 8 ms.

The same device grants RLIMIT_NICE = 40 (soft and hard) to the app and every
child, which is how Linux lets an unprivileged process lower its nice value:
setpriority succeeds down to 20 - rlim_cur, so nice -20 here. Realtime is not
granted (RLIMIT_RTPRIO is 0).

- CanSetNice(n): CAP_SYS_NICE, or an RLIMIT_NICE soft limit that reaches n
  (20 - rlim_cur <= n, or RLIM_INFINITY). SetNice uses it.
- main sets nice -20 on that path too. Threads created after it
  (gamescope-xwm, -wl, -kms, -wait, -vrmon, the vblank thread) inherit it.
  SetRealtime stays on CAP_SYS_NICE only.
- nice() only changes the calling thread, and two waiter threads are started
  by static constructors before main: g_ImageWaiter ("gamescope_img",
  steamcompmgr.cpp) and g_LibEisWaiter ("gamescope-eis", wlserver.cpp). They
  stayed at nice 0 (upstream has the same gap with CAP_SYS_NICE). On the RP6
  (SteamWorld Dig 2, 30 s live capture) gamescope_img, the thread that marks
  an Xwayland commit done, first ran 15.5 ms after the commit arrived, on a
  CPU 67 % busy with app threads; the commit was not done at the next wakeup
  and gamescope skipped a whole period. SetNice now also gives the new value
  to every other thread of the process that is still at the old one.
- RestoreNice no longer needs CAP_SYS_NICE: raising the value back needs no
  permission, and SetNice only records values when it lowered them. The game
  and the other children gamescope spawns still start at the nice gamescope
  was started with. Xwayland is forked by wlroots (xwayland/server.c), not
  through Process::SpawnProcess, so it inherits nice -20, as it does upstream
  on a desktop with CAP_SYS_NICE.
- The Vulkan queues are unchanged: VK_QUEUE_GLOBAL_PRIORITY_REALTIME is still
  requested only with CAP_SYS_NICE (rendervulkan.cpp CreateDevice, with its
  VK_ERROR_NOT_PERMITTED_KHR fallback), so without it both queues stay at
  regular priority as before.

Logs: "No CAP_SYS_NICE; RLIMIT_NICE permits nice -20 (threads at nice -20),
realtime compute queue and scheduling unavailable." once at start, instead of
the "falling back to regular-priority" line, and "Nice -20 also applied to
N thread(s) started before it was set." when earlier threads were moved.

diff --git a/src/Utils/Process.cpp b/src/Utils/Process.cpp
index 1a82f57..b94ae48 100644
--- a/src/Utils/Process.cpp
+++ b/src/Utils/Process.cpp
@@ -900,12 +900,70 @@ namespace gamescope::Process
 #endif
     }
 
+    static bool RlimitNiceAllows( int nNice )
+    {
+#if defined(__linux__)
+        // Without CAP_SYS_NICE, setpriority may still lower the nice value down to
+        // 20 - RLIMIT_NICE (the soft limit). This is how an unprivileged process is
+        // granted a better priority without the capability.
+        struct rlimit niceLimit{};
+        if ( getrlimit( RLIMIT_NICE, &niceLimit ) != 0 )
+            return false;
+
+        if ( niceLimit.rlim_cur == RLIM_INFINITY )
+            return true;
+
+        return 20 - static_cast<long long>( niceLimit.rlim_cur ) <= nNice;
+#else
+        return false;
+#endif
+    }
+
+    bool CanSetNice( int nNice )
+    {
+        return HasCapSysNice() || RlimitNiceAllows( nNice );
+    }
+
+    // nice() only changes the calling thread. Threads that already exist keep the
+    // nice they were started with, and some are started by static constructors
+    // before main gets here (g_ImageWaiter "gamescope_img", "gamescope-eis").
+    // Give every other thread still at the old value the new one.
+    static void ApplyNiceToEarlierThreads( int nOldNice, int nNewNice )
+    {
+#if defined(__linux__)
+        DIR *pTaskDir = opendir( "/proc/self/task" );
+        if ( !pTaskDir )
+            return;
+        defer( closedir( pTaskDir ) );
+
+        const pid_t nSelf = gettid();
+        int nApplied = 0;
+        while ( struct dirent *pEntry = readdir( pTaskDir ) )
+        {
+            std::optional<pid_t> onTid = Parse<pid_t>( pEntry->d_name );
+            if ( !onTid || *onTid == nSelf )
+                continue;
+
+            errno = 0;
+            int nThreadNice = getpriority( PRIO_PROCESS, *onTid );
+            if ( errno != 0 || nThreadNice != nOldNice )
+                continue;
+
+            if ( setpriority( PRIO_PROCESS, *onTid, nNewNice ) == 0 )
+                nApplied++;
+        }
+
+        if ( nApplied )
+            s_ProcessLog.infof( "Nice %d also applied to %d thread(s) started before it was set.", nNewNice, nApplied );
+#endif
+    }
+
     std::optional<int> g_oOldNice;
     std::optional<int> g_oNewNice;
     void SetNice( int nNice )
     {
 #if defined(__linux__)
-        if ( !HasCapSysNice() )
+        if ( !CanSetNice( nNice ) )
             return;
 
         errno = 0;
@@ -920,6 +978,9 @@ namespace gamescope::Process
         if ( nNewNice != -1 || errno == 0 )
         {
             g_oNewNice = nNewNice;
+
+            if ( g_oOldNice && *g_oOldNice != nNewNice )
+                ApplyNiceToEarlierThreads( *g_oOldNice, nNewNice );
         }
 #endif
     }
@@ -927,9 +988,9 @@ namespace gamescope::Process
     void RestoreNice()
     {
 #if defined(__linux__)
-        if ( !HasCapSysNice() )
-            return;
-
+        // Raising the nice value back needs no permission, and SetNice only
+        // records values when it was allowed to lower it (by CAP_SYS_NICE or
+        // RLIMIT_NICE), so children must be restored either way.
         if ( !g_oOldNice || !g_oNewNice )
             return;
 
